A||||7||Wolfhound||Vinyl Headliner||||1||
Z||000000||Wolfhound||07-17-2001||08:27 AM||||someone recently mentioned using vinyl for a headliner material. Well I decided to give it a shot so I could see past the stalactites in my Gold SE.<P>I removed the liner backing from another car so I could reduce the down time for the SE. I used a full Quart of contact cement double coating both surfaces. The solvent in the contact cement seemed to discolor and soften the vinyl but I continued on as I was committed at this point.<P>After bonding I allowed it to sit overnight and it cured out nice and flat. Yesterday I worked the outside edges over and used super weather strip adhesive to bond the vinyl to the backside of the backing.<P>Removed the original liner and instaled the new one. I layed a small bead of the super weather srtip adhesive to the lower edge of the sunroof opening grove and worked the molding in triming as I went around the edge.<P>Here's the result. I had to use flash which causes discoloration but the vinyl is a near exact match to the leather seats.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/vliner.JPG"> <BR> Cost was about 15 dollars.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cheever2||07-17-2001||11:55 AM||||I'm impressed.  Looks great.  I'll prob'ly try that when I do my GT w/sunroof in a month or two.<BR><P>------------------<BR>- <I>Chuck Hodges</I> -<BR><A HREF="http://home.houston.rr.com/fieropartsrelay" TARGET=_blank> Fiero Parts Relay</A> administrator||||reg||1
